Z||000000||jailer||08-23-99||09:47 AM||||Hope that someone can help me with this.<br>Have a 1984 2m4. Problem is this. When I start the car, it stalls right away. Step on the gas and the engine bogs down, tap the gas pedal and I can work the rpms up but not keep them there.  Getting a heavy gas smell from the exhaust. Once the car runs long enough to warm up, the engine runs just fine except for a slight miss, barely noticeable. This problem only seems to happen when the temperature is cool and/or damp outside. Usually don't have a problem starting when the outside temp is hot.<br>P.S. Engine has 154,000+ miles.<br>Just recently changed the fuel filter, IAC valve, plugs & wires, cap & rotor, nothing helped.
Z||000001||DJRice||08-23-99||10:29 AM||||You should probably check the timing. <br>
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||08-23-99||10:47 AM||||Disconnect the tach filter and see if the problem remains. You can also check/replace the ignition module.<p>But to be honest, this is one of those "could be anything" problems... <IMG SRC="/ubb/frown.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>Ogre? Any ideas?<br>
Z||000003||theogre||08-23-99||11:36 AM||||check the injector in the TBI, and the TPS module. It's flooding. A sticking injector or a TPS that is reporting the wrong pedle angle can cause this. Make sure the MAP sensor vacuum line is attached. The ECM uses only the MAP and TPS to figure out mix until the O2 sensor warms up.
Z||000004||lowCG||08-23-99||11:56 AM||||You can test the coolant temperature sensor that tells the brain how much fuel is required during colder temperatures,using an ohmeter.Approximate values should be;<br>160Deg F-------450 Ohms<br>40Deg F--------7,500 Ohms
Z||000005||ChadMan||08-24-99||07:48 AM||||DEFINITELY check out Ogre's MAP sensor tip!  I once forgot to reattach the MAP sensor vacuum line and the car did the exact same thing you're talking about until I noticed my mistake.  The car will run really, really rich if this line is disconnected.
Z||000006||FieroSTETZ||08-31-99||09:08 PM||||i am having the same problem, except with a 6 cylinder, except it won't start and stay that way for more than 4 seconds. i have tried re-timing it and everything. any input would be groovy
Z||000007||Greg Piet||08-31-99||10:48 PM||||I had a similar problem recently.<br>I was running in diagnostic mode only @ 1500 rpm.<p>Finally the problem was that the remanufactured distributor magnetic ring was not pressed firmly, and the ring literally moved upward (you should see some slots above the ring parallel to the axis of the rotating shaft)  This resulted in the vehicles timing gear literally sliding up w/o disengaging, thus your timing goes out of whack.  timing in diagnostic mode will look fine, but out of diag mode it runs like garbage and it is necessary to feather the gas to keep the car running if it runs at all.  If it is a sensor, the car should run in diagnostic mode!  It uses default settings, and activates all solenoids which will let the car run (not well, but idle at least) You can easily check the TPS, by treating it as a potentiometer or disconnecting it so the computer uses the default idle value, checking the IAC valve by listening for clicks, or comparing the ohmage of pins 1-2 and pins 3-4 compared to one that is properly functioning (I tested one at the auto store in front of the clerk).<br>I ahve been told that the coolant sensor<br>cuses problems and is known for being a problem, but there is a site wheer it lists the ohmage for particular temperatures<br>Here is a useful place<br>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/osg.html#diag
